ould  properly relate to politics and it's a  certain way of understanding politics  and how politics should properly relate  to religion so from the religious side I  think they're really sort of three  things that I would single out what one  of them is again kind of activism and  this is the notion that that religion  imposes a duty on believers to try to  alleviate suffering and to improve the  world in some way so it's not sort of  the world is a horrible sinful place  let's turn away from it  I'll let's retreat it into our own  communities and and wait for you know  the return of Jesus there's nothing like  that  another thing involves is a certain  measure of humility and I think this and  the in this Puritan tradition comes from  a kind of a profound sense of the  sinfulness and limitations of human  beings that though people are devoted to  trying to improve the world that the  world is in some ways a kind is not  easily moldable simply because of the  way that human beings are and the last  thing that I would emphasize is  engagement which I think follows from  the previous two which is a kind of a  willingness to open up with and  deliberate with people who think  differently with non-believers or people  who might you might consider to be  heterodox in one way or not  and that is certainly something that  comes after the Puritans because they  would not have been open to that kind of  engagement from the political side I  think I've touched on some of the main  points already but the the main thing  that I main things I would emphasize  here are an emphasis on engagement and  restraint so that we see self-government  we see self-government as an experiment  that we're engaged in collectively with  other people that requires us to sort of  open up and deliberate with people but  also to be restrained and civil in our  deliberations with those other people so  in some civil religion as I understand  it is a certain way of understanding the  relationship between religion and  politics it has a certain understanding  of the way in which religious  institutions and political institutions  and community should relate to one  another it's also refers to a certain  kind of tradition within American  politics  and finally it's a certain kind of  ethical or normative position that we  take in our dealings with others in  politics so can you give us some  examples of civil religion that we might  see today I can and the one that I would  point to actually is a presidential  campaign of Barack Obama which is what  actually got me interested and looking  at this subject and reexamining it again  here I would point in particular to the  speech on race that he gave in in  Philadelphia following the uproar around  the gerra that Jeremiah Wright tapes you  may remember that the the opening line  of that speech which was delivered at  the convention the content of the  Constitution Center in Philadelphia was  taken directly from the preamble of the  Constitution we the people of the United  States in order to to form a more  perfect union and immediately the place  and the the  the the Oratory already evokes two of  the most sacred things within American  history the the Constitution which is  one of the two most sacred documents in  American political history and of course  this is taking place across the street  from Independence Hall where the  Constitution was actually written none  of which I'm sure was was a coincidence  and what he's talking about then is a  more perfect more perfect union between  between the races and was interesting  here is that he immediately shifts  within a few lines in the speech also to  into a kind of a Puritan register as  well here echoing Abraham Lincoln's  second inaugural address and also the  writings of Frederick Douglass  he talks about the Constitution as as a  document that was flawed by what he  calls the the stain of the original sin  of slavery and so using this very  explicitly religious language which also  sort of evokes this notion of a covenant  that's unfulfilled that that there was a  certain kind of political covenant that  was embodied in the Declaration of  Independence and the Constitution a  commitment to equal rights for all  people which was then in violated  immediately by the recognition of  slavery in the Constitution three-fifths  rule so on and so forth  now what's interesting here though is  that he's not just evoking the language  of of American civil religion there is  important way in which he is also  broadening and transforming it it's  important to remember here that neither  the Puritans nor the founding fathers  were exact or exactly Emancipation s or  abolitionists certainly you know  Jefferson you know for whatever you know  his personal dalliance is and history  may have been was no champion of  race-mixing in fact had you know some  rather shocking things to say actually  about both African Americans and Native  Americans and the same thing could be  said actually about many of the most  prominent Puritan preachers and  intellectuals and mathers for example  who were also not proto abolitionist by  any means so there's a way in which what  he's doing here is he's he's going back  to these original values that are  embodied in the Constitution and he's  saying that this is the sort of an  unfolding experiment in which were sort  of trying to live up to the promises  that we made at the founding of the  United States perhaps not fully  understanding exactly what it was that  we were promising and that's another  sense in which I think this is a bit  like the the covenant in the Bible that  this was sort of an unfolding  relationship a sort of unfolding  agreement between the ancient Israelites  and and their God so I guess what this  this makes clear is that there is also a  kind of a political dimension to the  project to I'm you know given that the  civil religion seems to be making a  comeback as one way of understanding how  politics and religion should be related  in the United States I think it's  important that that we begin to reflect  on what that tradition is how has  changed over time and what would be a  defensible version of civil religion in  contemporary in the contemporary United  States which is more religiously and  culturally diverse than ever and that I  think actually is one of the great  challenges in thinking about civil  religion that is that civil religion can  no longer be just a kind of a minimal  watered down kind of Protestantism or or  even monotheism it has to be something  that can embrace believers and  unbelievers alike the yesenin theose  alike and that's also part of what I'm  trying to think through right  on this project okay let's talk a little  bit about the history how did the term  civil religion originated the person who  coined the term civil religion is the  French political philosopher  jean-jacques Rousseau in the social  contract and the way in which it then  really entered into into kind of social  science discourse is through through the  work of Robert Bellah emeritus professor  of sociology at the University of  California Berkeley who wrote a very  famous essay called the American civil  religion in 1967 which provoked an  enormous uproar that lasted lasted for  twenty years and which maybe I'm in a  way trying to stir up again mm-hmm what  is it about civil religion that  interests here why are you studying it  researching it  well full disclosure part of it is that  Robert Bellah was was my doctoral  adviser and I think it was because of  having gotten to know his thinking and  having read a lot of his writings on  civil religion when I was a graduate  student that when I heard Obama speaking  in the race speech and other speeches  you know I heard the echoes of King and  Kennedy I heard the echoes of Lincoln  and Douglas and I immediately connected  it to this tradition of American  American civil religion but of course  it