nt now obviously  I would call that not civil religion  that Civic religion that's an  understanding of the relationship  between religion politics which sees in  a way religion as being subservient to  the ends of the state and really not  even different in a way from from the  state  Machiavelli is often seen as another  sort of big figure and the in the  development of civil religion but he has  not surprising the more Machiavelli in  take on what role religion should play  in supporting self-government he sees it  as perhaps a necessary evil as a  convenient instrument that can be used  by rulers or ruling classes to instill  certain virtues in in the masses so you  know self-discipline and bravery and so  on and so forth and again this sort of  purely instrumental relationship almost  kind of hypocritical relationship is not  one that I would sort of morally morally  espouse finally there's were so who  coins the term but he's really almost  what his vision is kind of a sort of a  platonic neo civic religion so what he  argues for is again a kind of a state  established obligatory called is the  kind of a watered-down deism is what he  actually actually has in mind with  similar penalties of people who are  nonconformist being expelled so really  where I part ways with all of these  traditions is and vociferously defending  the rights of conscious and of religious  freedom which I see as integral integral  to what the United States has been as as  as an experiment and as a democracy and  really in some ways I see the rights of  consciousness the fount of all  understandings of Rights the rights to  free speech the right to assembly these  are all things which in a way are  implied by by the rights of conscious  when they're really fully understood  what about the two social scientists  Durkheim and Bela Durkheim is like many  European intellectuals of his generation  was at a profit of secularization he  imagined that religion would necessarily  appear as modernity unfolded  unfortunately he was or fortunately he  was wrong about that depending on your  point of view I think it's us is a very  modern society but it's not at least in  terms of the convictions of its of his  citizens a particularly secular society  on the contrary and it's not at all  clear that that the rest of the world is  going more the way of secular Europe I  think actually the United States is  probably more the model for what the  religious world to look like years from  now so that's his vision was that civil  religion would replace traditional  religion but that was based on the  assumption that traditional religion was  going away which I think has turned out  to poo has proven to be incorrect  now Robert Bellah certainly did not  share in this view that secularization  would triumph that religion would  disappear but he was in many ways still  very much informed by by Durkheim  believing in a way that every society to  be a cohesive society had to have some  kind of central moral consensus around  which around which it would cohere and  he argued that civic religion was that  consensus within within the United  States where I think he perhaps went a  little bit off path was and not seeing  that there are the rival ways of  thinking about how religion and politics  should be related that it played an  enormous role in in American history and  I've already alluded to two of them one  of them is liberal secularism and or  total separation ISM and this is the  notion that religion politics absolutely  shouldn't absolutely shouldn't mix  religious arguments have no place in the  public realm religious actors have no  place in public life religious symbols  have no place in public spaces  and this view was already there in  Jefferson's lis writings its Jefferson  after all who invents this famous phrase  about the the wall of separation between  church and state which then becomes kind  of a lens which a lot of thinking about  legal thinking about chu