nity unfolded  unfortunately he was or fortunately he  was wrong about that depending on your  point of view I think it's us is a very  modern society but it's not at least in  terms of the convictions of its of his  citizens a particularly secular society  on the contrary and it's not at all  clear that that the rest of the world is  going more the way of secular Europe I  think actually the United States is  probably more the model for what the  religious world to look like years from  now so that's his vision was that civil  religion would replace traditional  religion but that was based on the  assumption that traditional religion was  going away which I think has turned out  to poo has proven to be incorrect  now Robert Bellah certainly did not  share in this view that secularization  would triumph that religion would  disappear but he was in many ways still  very much informed by by Durkheim  believing in a way that every society to  be a cohesive society had to have some  kind of central moral consensus around  which around which it would cohere and  he argued that civic religion was that  consensus within within the United  States where I think he perhaps went a  little bit off path was and not seeing  that there are the rival ways of  thinking about how religion and politics  should be related that it played an  enormous role in in American history and  I've already alluded to two of them one  of them is liberal secularism and or  total separation ISM and this is the  notion that religion politics absolutely  shouldn't absolutely shouldn't mix  religious arguments have no place in the  public realm religious actors have no  place in public life religious symbols  have no place in public spaces  and this view was already there in  Jefferson's lis writings its Jefferson  after all who invents this famous phrase  about the the wall of separation between  church and state which then becomes kind  of a lens which a lot of thinking about  legal thinking about church and state  then is shaped during the during the  second half of the 20th century another  one is that's very important is  religious nationalism and by this I mean  the view that the u.s. is and should be  a Christian nation and the sort of  implicit view the people who are not  Christians or and even Christians of a  particular kind have no place in the  United States and I think one of the  more remarkable statements in this line  is a statement made by George W Bush  some years ago when I asked whether he  thought an atheist could be a good  America and he expressed very serious  doubts and there are sort of more  strident versions of this that are very  much out there and alive in certain  conservative Christian circles you know  people who really believe that the only  way to sort of restore the health of the  United States is to kind of completely  Christianize it again they're even  extremely radical though fringe versions  of this that people refer to as the ahna  me which believe that that the United  States notes it has to be subjected to  Old Testament biblical law you know  adulterers have to be stoned and you  know the whole nine yards this is very  much of a fringe view but as sort of a  softer version of this that somehow  there's no place for non-believers or  non-christians of a particular kind  within the United States is very much  out there and I see civil religion as as  a way of perhaps our bridging and  overcoming this conflict between liberal  secular and religious nationalists which  I think my view is a lot of what the  cultural Wars of the last 25 years have  really been about okay well thank you  very much professor course  for sharing some of your research with  us thank you for more information about  professor Gorski and his research please  visit our website at Yale edu backslash  Macmillan report be sure to join us  again for another segment of the  Macmillan report made possible by the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Centre for  International and area studies at Yale
 

